// Copyright (c) 2026 Jurjen Stellingwerff
// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// Plan 26 phase L0 — is ANY mover tick-length independent?
//
// ── The one claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// > **A rate is a rate: a mover covers the same ground per SECOND
// > whatever the timestep is.**
//
// `@X058` released the constraint that made a tick a hex, so the
// timestep is a free choice and `TICK_SECONDS` is what HOLDS it at
// 667 ms rather than what forces it.  That freedom is worth exactly
// what the movers can survive — and `plans/22` cashes it in.
//
// This file is the instrument that asks.  It is plan 26's L0, and it
// is the only phase of that plan that pays off if the rest is never
// built.
//
// ── What it found, and it was a DEFECT ──────────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **The enemies and the crew held their rate at every timestep,
// and the PLAYER did not.**  Over one simulated minute, at seven tick
// lengths from 667 ms down to 33 ms:
//
// | mover | 667 | 500 | 333 | 200 | 100 | 50 | 33 ms | true |
// |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
// | miner 1.0 hex/s   |  60 |  60 |  60 |  60 |  60 |  60 |  60 |  60 |
// | robot 1.5 hex/s   |  90 |  90 |  90 |  90 |  90 |  90 |  90 |  90 |
// | scout 2.5 hex/s   | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 |
// | helper 2.5 hex/s  | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 | 150 |
// | **vehicle 3.0**   | 180 | **120** | 180 | **0** | **0** | **0** | **0** | 180 |
// | **boost 6.0**     | 360 | 360 | 360 | **300** | **0** | **0** | **0** | 360 |
//
// `vehicle_hexes_this_tick` TRUNCATED and carried nothing, where
// `enemy_bank` and `helper_bank` kept the remainder:
//
//     (vehicle_speed(v) * tick_seconds) as integer?    — vehicle.loft
//
// and `Vehicle` had no `progress` field to put one in.  At a fifth of
// a second `3.0 * 0.2` is `0.6000000000000001`, which truncates to
// **nothing** — so the player sat still for a whole minute while every
// robot on the map walked at its documented speed.  Recorded as
// `@D003`, measured as `@M030`.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ AND THEN L2 FIXED IT, WHICH IS WHY THIS FILE READS 180 ───
//
// Plan 26 L2 gave the vehicle the bank it never had, over L1's integer
// clock, and deleted both mover epsilons with it.  **Every row of the
// table above is now 180 or 360 at all seven tick lengths, and the
// four functions in § The vehicle does too were RED on the phase that
// changed them** — which is what a tripwire is for (`tests/23_k1`'s
// "not yet" sense, and `@X064`: a phase that changed the thing it
// measured would have measured nothing).
//
// ⚠ **The instrument did not change and that is the point.**  Nothing
// below was re-aimed to make the fix pass: the sweep, the corridor and
// the positive controls are L0's, and only the ASSERTED numbers moved.
// A gate rewritten alongside the code it gates has verified nothing —
// which is `tests/golden`'s own rule, one layer up.
//
// ⚠ The one shape that DID change is the axis's type.  Tick lengths
// are integer base units now (1/3 µs, `tick_clock.loft`), so
// `TICK_STEP_UNITS / 3` is an exact third of a tick where
// `TICK_SECONDS / 3.0` was not — and every one of the seven lengths
// divides one simulated minute exactly, so the sweep still needs no
// tolerance.
//
// ── Why nothing in the corpus had seen it, which is the reusable
//    half ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **Three accidents in a row, and each one is a gate looking
// straight past it.**
//
// 1. **Both shipped vehicle speeds are exact at the shipped tick.**
//    `3.0 * (1/1.5)` is 2.0 to the bit and `6.0 * (1/1.5)` is 4.0, so
//    at 667 ms the truncation takes nothing.  That is `@M014`'s
//    accident — *1.5 hex/s is a speed at which the guard cannot fire*
//    — arriving on a different mover.
// 2. **The half-tick the corpus already sweeps is exact too.**
//    `tests/23_k2a::test_the_mover_agrees_at_half_the_timestep` runs
//    the shortened timestep dryopea owns, and `3.0 * (1/3)` is 1.0 to
//    the bit, so the axis is right and the reading is blind.
// 3. **The one gate with a tick length that WOULD see it drives the
//    wrong mover.**  `23_k2a`'s tenth-tick case is 0.0667 s, where
//    `3.0 * 0.0667` is 0.2 and truncates to zero — and it banks an
//    ENEMY, never a vehicle.
//
// ⚠ That third one is `tests/11_f8`'s markerless-world trap, one plan
// on: *a cost gate over a world with none of the thing you changed is
// not a gate*.  Here the corpus has the right AXIS and the wrong
// SUBJECT, which is harder to see, because the sweep looks thorough.
//
// ⚠ And `@M013` — the sweep this defect most resembles — cannot reach
// it from any direction: it varies the SPEED, at one tick length,
// through `enemy_bank`, which carries.  **The axis is the tick length
// and the subject is the whole roster; you need both.**
//
// ── What is deliberately NOT here ───────────────────────────────
//
// **No fix — as SHIPPED at L0.**  L0 was the instrument; the bank the
// vehicle never had is L2, and L1's integer clock came first.  The
// four functions in § The vehicle does too were a *defect record*:
// they asserted the wrong numbers so that the day they were right,
// this file went RED and named what changed.  ⚠ They were a "not yet"
// pin in `tests/23_k1`'s sense — something a later phase must BREAK —
// and L2 broke all four, which is the record that the fix was a fix.
//
// **No scenario through `wave_tick`.**  It reads `TICK_SECONDS` as a
// constant, so a whole-game run at another timestep is a production
// change, and `plans/26` § What this plan does NOT build refuses to
// shorten the tick here precisely because it would confound every
// reading above.  The movers all take their timestep as a PARAMETER
// already, which is what makes this measurable today.
//
// **No timers.**  `tower_repair_tick`, `helper_recover_tick` and the
// lull are a different family (`plans/26` § The invariant) and they
// are L3's.  ⚠ They are still float seconds after L2, and
// `clock_seconds_from_units` is the one named seam they come through.

#cwd

use dryopea;

const PALETTE: text = "examples/palette.json";
const GRASS:   integer = 5;

// One simulated minute, in base time units — which every tick length
// below divides exactly, so "the same ground per second" is an exact
// integer for every speed in the game and the sweep needs no
// tolerance.
//
// ⚠ **`60 * CLOCK_UNITS_PER_SECOND`, spelled out** rather than
// computed from `SECONDS: float = 60.0`, because L2's whole subject is
// that a duration is an integer count of a base unit.  A test that
// derived its axis through a float would be measuring the thing it is
// asserting about.
const MINUTE_UNITS: integer = 180000000;

// ⚠ A FILE-SCOPE `const vector` holding a negative number is EMPTY
// ([loft#955]) and a loop over an empty vector holds vacuously, so the
// sweep is built inside a function.  These are all positive and would
// be safe — the rule is followed anyway, because the failure is silent
// and the next reader adding a row should not have to know.
fn tick_lengths() -> vector<integer> {
    // 667 / 500 / 333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms, in base units.  The
    // first is today's tick, the last is moros's 30 Hz — the two real
    // timesteps in the ecosystem, with the interesting ground between
    // them.
    //
    // ⚠ **All seven are exact**, which is a property of the base unit
    // rather than of this list: 1/30 of a second is 100 000 thirds of
    // a microsecond, where it is not a whole number of microseconds at
    // all (`tick_clock.loft` § THE BASE UNIT IS A CHOICE, `@X079`).
    [TICK_STEP_UNITS, 1500000, TICK_STEP_UNITS / 2, 600000,
     300000, 150000, 100000]
}

fn as_u8(n: integer) -> u8 {
    n as u8? ?? 0
}

// How many ticks of `dt` make one simulated minute.
fn ticks_in_a_minute(dt: integer) -> integer {
    (MINUTE_UNITS / dt) ?? 0
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The instrument: hexes released over one minute, one door per mover
//
// ⚠ Every one of these goes through the SHIPPED release function.  A
// reimplementation of the arithmetic here would agree with itself and
// say nothing — `tests/23_k2a`'s rule, and this file needs it more,
// because the whole finding is that three of these do not agree.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn enemy_minute(speed: float, dt: integer) -> integer {
    em_e = Enemy { q: 40, r: 0, kind: as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR),
                   heading: 3 as u8? ?? 0 as u8, alive: true,
                   taken: 0.0, stand: timer_new(), bank: bank_new() };
    em_got = 0;
    for _ in 0..ticks_in_a_minute(dt) { em_got = em_got + enemy_bank(em_e, speed, dt); }
    em_got
}

fn helper_minute(dt: integer) -> integer {
    hm_h   = helper_new(40, 0);
    hm_got = 0;
    for _ in 0..ticks_in_a_minute(dt) { hm_got = hm_got + helper_bank(hm_h, dt); }
    hm_got
}

fn vehicle_minute(boosting: boolean, dt: integer) -> integer {
    // ⚠ The boost timer is armed directly rather than through
    // `vehicle_boost`, because the real boost burns out after
    // `VEHICLE_BOOST_DURATION_UNITS` and this measures the ARITHMETIC
    // of a boosting vehicle over a whole minute.  What burns it is
    // `vehicle_burn`, which `vehicle_bank` does not call.
    vm_v = vehicle_empty();
    vehicle_place(vm_v, 40, 0);
    if boosting { timer_arm(vm_v.boost, MINUTE_UNITS * 2); }
    vm_got = 0;
    for _ in 0..ticks_in_a_minute(dt) {
        vm_got = vm_got + vehicle_bank(vm_v, dt);
    }
    vm_got
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One reading per tick length, as one string
//
// ⚠⚠ **The whole sweep is ONE assertion, and that is plan 25 M1's
// finding applied rather than restated.**  loft abandons a test
// function at its first failed assertion, so seven asserts in a row
// can only ever report the earliest one that breaks — and the break
// this file exists to catch (L2 giving the vehicle its bank) moves
// FIVE of the seven at once.  Measured: asserted row by row it named
// *"500 ms loses a third of it"* and said nothing about the four zeros.
//
// ⚠ Splitting into seven functions is M1's own fix and is the wrong
// one here: seven rows of one sweep are one claim about one mover, not
// seven independent counts on seven fixtures.  So they stay one
// assertion and the MESSAGE carries the whole profile — which reads,
// on the day L2 lands, as `180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180`.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn enemy_profile(speed: float) -> text {
    ep_out   = "";
    ep_first = true;
    for ep_dt in tick_lengths() {
        if !ep_first { ep_out = ep_out + " / "; }
        ep_out   = ep_out + "{enemy_minute(speed, ep_dt)}";
        ep_first = false;
    }
    ep_out
}

fn helper_profile() -> text {
    hp_out   = "";
    hp_first = true;
    for hp_dt in tick_lengths() {
        if !hp_first { hp_out = hp_out + " / "; }
        hp_out   = hp_out + "{helper_minute(hp_dt)}";
        hp_first = false;
    }
    hp_out
}

fn vehicle_profile(boosting: boolean) -> text {
    vp_out   = "";
    vp_first = true;
    for vp_dt in tick_lengths() {
        if !vp_first { vp_out = vp_out + " / "; }
        vp_out   = vp_out + "{vehicle_minute(boosting, vp_dt)}";
        vp_first = false;
    }
    vp_out
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The end-to-end fixture: a corridor long enough that a healthy mover
// arrives inside the minute with room to spare
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn pal() -> vector<GroundType> {
    load_palette(PALETTE)
}

// 40 hexes of grass along r = 0, from (40, 0) down to the core.
fn corridor() -> PaintedWorld {
    c_pw = painted_empty();
    for c_q in 0..=40 { paint(c_pw, c_q, 0, as_u8(GRASS)); }
    c_pw
}

// Drive the player west down the corridor for a minute of `dt`, and
// answer the column it stopped in.  0 = it arrived.
fn player_drove(dt: integer) -> integer {
    pd_pal = pal();
    pd_pw  = corridor();
    pd_v   = vehicle_empty();
    vehicle_place(pd_v, 40, 0);
    vehicle_drive(pd_v, 0, 0);
    for _ in 0..ticks_in_a_minute(dt) {
        vehicle_tick(pd_v, pd_pal, pd_pw, height_layer_empty(), dt);
    }
    pd_v.q
}

// The four tick lengths the corridor is walked at.  Fewer than the
// arithmetic sweep because a walk is a whole flow field and 1800 ticks
// of a real mover; these four are the two exact-by-accident cases and
// the two that lose everything.
fn walk_lengths() -> vector<integer> {
    [TICK_STEP_UNITS, 1500000, 600000, 300000]
}

// The same corridor, walked by a regular robot at 1.5 hex/s.  It needs
// 26.7 s of the minute, so it arrives at every timestep or the FIXTURE
// is broken rather than the mover.
fn robot_walked(dt: integer) -> integer {
    rw_pal = pal();
    rw_pw  = corridor();
    rw_e   = Enemy { q: 40, r: 0, kind: as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR),
                     heading: 3 as u8? ?? 0 as u8, alive: true,
                     taken: 0.0, stand: timer_new(), bank: bank_new() };
    rw_f   = flow_build(rw_pal, rw_pw, height_layer_empty(),
                        as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR), Hex { q: 0, r: 0 });
    rw_d   = flow_desire(rw_pal, rw_pw, height_layer_empty(), Hex { q: 0, r: 0 });
    for _ in 0..ticks_in_a_minute(dt) {
        enemy_tick(rw_e, rw_pal, rw_pw, height_layer_empty(), rw_f, rw_d,
                   occupancy_empty(), blocker_empty(), dt);
    }
    rw_e.q
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The banked movers hold their rate — the POSITIVE control
//
// ⚠ These are what make the four failures below the VEHICLE's rather
// than the instrument's.  `@M022`'s generic control asks whether a
// gate can produce a non-trivial reading at all; here the stronger
// form is available, because the same instrument reads both answers on
// the same axis in the same file.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn test_a_slow_enemy_holds_its_rate_at_every_timestep() {
    // A miner is SLOWER than one hex a tick at every timestep in the
    // sweep, so every hex it covers is one the carry paid for.
    aeh_got = enemy_profile(enemy_speed(as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_MINER)));
    assert(aeh_got == "60 / 60 / 60 / 60 / 60 / 60 / 60",
           "a miner at 1.0 hex/s covers 60 hexes a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms — got {aeh_got}");
}

fn test_a_regular_enemy_holds_its_rate_at_every_timestep() {
    // ⚠ 1.5 hex/s is the speed the tick was DERIVED from, so this row
    // is the one that would be exact even with no carry at all — it is
    // here as the sweep's own null case rather than as evidence.
    are_got = enemy_profile(enemy_speed(as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR)));
    assert(are_got == "90 / 90 / 90 / 90 / 90 / 90 / 90",
           "a robot at 1.5 hex/s covers 90 hexes a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms — got {are_got}");
}

fn test_a_fast_enemy_holds_its_rate_at_every_timestep() {
    // A scout is faster than one hex a tick at 667 ms and slower at
    // every shorter one, so it crosses the boundary the truncation
    // lives on and keeps its rate across it.
    afe_got = enemy_profile(enemy_speed(as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_SCOUT)));
    assert(afe_got == "150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150",
           "a scout at 2.5 hex/s covers 150 hexes a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms — got {afe_got}");
}

fn test_the_helper_holds_its_rate_at_every_timestep() {
    // ⚠ The crew is the case that PROVES the invariant is reachable
    // rather than merely true of enemies: `helper_bank` reads its speed
    // from a constant where `enemy_bank` takes an argument (`@X060`),
    // and 2.5 hex/s is a speed at which the epsilon really does fire.
    thh_got = helper_profile();
    assert(thh_got == "150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150 / 150",
           "a helper at 2.5 hex/s covers 150 hexes a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms — got {thh_got}");
}

fn test_the_sweep_is_not_degenerate() {
    // ⚠ The generic control, and it earned its place while this file
    // was being written: the first run of the probe reported EVERY
    // mover frozen at every timestep, which read exactly like a finding
    // and was an empty palette.  A sweep whose loop never runs asserts
    // nothing at all ([loft#955]), and a fixture nothing can move in
    // reports the world's defect as the mover's.
    tsd_lengths = tick_lengths();
    assert(len(tsd_lengths) == 7,
           "the sweep has seven tick lengths, got {len(tsd_lengths)}");
    tsd_ticks = ticks_in_a_minute(tsd_lengths[6] ?? 1);
    assert(tsd_ticks == 1800,
           "and the shortest of them is 1800 ticks of work, got {tsd_ticks}");

    // And the corridor is a place a mover can actually travel.
    tsd_pal = pal();
    assert(len(tsd_pal) > 0, "the palette loaded, got {len(tsd_pal)} kinds");
    assert(can_stand(tsd_pal, corridor(), height_layer_empty(),
                     as_u8(ENEMY_KIND_REGULAR), 40, 0),
           "and the far end of the corridor is ground a robot can stand on");
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ⚠⚠ The vehicle does TOO, since plan 26 L2 — `@D003` closed
//
// Everything below asserted a WRONG number on purpose until L2 landed.
// It was the defect's record and L2's tripwire, and the phase that
// gave the vehicle its bank turned every one of these RED before
// turning it green on the true number — which is the only way a fix
// and its gate can be told apart.
//
// ⚠ The FUNCTION NAMES are kept as they were written, and that is
// deliberate: `test_the_vehicle_loses_ground_as_the_tick_shortens`
// naming a claim that no longer holds is how the file says what it
// used to say.  Renaming them would leave `@D003` with no record but
// prose.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn test_the_vehicle_loses_ground_as_the_tick_shortens() {
    // ⚠ It does not any more.  Was `180 / 120 / 180 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0`.
    // 500 ms was the insidious reading — the player still moved, at two
    // thirds of its documented speed, which reads as a feel complaint
    // rather than as arithmetic.  The four zeros did not read as
    // anything: the player was simply gone.
    tvl_got = vehicle_profile(false);
    assert(tvl_got == "180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180 / 180",
           "the player covers 180 hexes a minute at 667 / 500 / 333 / "
           + "200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms.  Was 180 / 120 / 180 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0 "
           + "before plan 26 L2 (@D003).  Got {tvl_got}");
}

fn test_a_boosting_vehicle_loses_it_too() {
    // ⚠ Nor does this.  Was `360 / 360 / 360 / 300 / 0 / 0 / 0` — twice
    // the speed bought one more tick length and no more, because a
    // faster mover was not a safer one, only one whose cliff was
    // further down.
    abv_got = vehicle_profile(true);
    assert(abv_got == "360 / 360 / 360 / 360 / 360 / 360 / 360",
           "a boosting player covers 360 hexes a minute at 667 / 500 / "
           + "333 / 200 / 100 / 50 / 33 ms.  Was 360 / 360 / 360 / 300 / "
           + "0 / 0 / 0 before plan 26 L2 (@D003).  Got {abv_got}");
}

fn test_boost_outruns_cruise_by_an_infinite_factor() {
    // ⚠ It outruns it by TWO, which is what a boost is worth.  The
    // reading this function was written for is the sharpest thing L0
    // found: at a fifth of a second a boosting player crossed 300 hexes
    // a minute and a cruising one crossed NONE, so the boost was worth
    // an unbounded multiple of its 2x.  **A truncation does not scale a
    // rate down, it reorders which rates exist** — and that is why the
    // fix had to be a bank rather than a better rounding.
    boi_cruise = vehicle_minute(false, 600000);
    boi_boost  = vehicle_minute(true,  600000);
    assert(boi_cruise == 180 && boi_boost == 360,
           "at 200 ms cruise is {boi_cruise} and boost is {boi_boost}, "
           + "so the ratio is exactly 2x.  Was 0 and 300 before plan 26 L2");
}

fn test_the_player_does_not_move_at_all_below_a_quarter_second() {
    // The same defect through the real mover rather than the
    // arithmetic — `vehicle_tick` over painted ground, pointed at the
    // core, for a whole simulated minute.
    //
    // ⚠ 500 ms ARRIVED even while broken, which is why the sweep above
    // is not redundant with this: 40 hexes at one a tick is 120 hexes
    // of travel in 120 ticks, so a mover losing a third of its rate
    // still got there and the corridor could not see it.  **A journey
    // with slack hides a rate error; only the count does not** — and
    // that is the half of this file worth keeping now the defect is
    // closed.
    tpd_out   = "";
    tpd_first = true;
    for tpd_dt in walk_lengths() {
        if !tpd_first { tpd_out = tpd_out + " / "; }
        tpd_out   = tpd_out + "{player_drove(tpd_dt)}";
        tpd_first = false;
    }
    assert(tpd_out == "0 / 0 / 0 / 0",
           "the column the player stopped in after a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 200 / 100 ms, driving from (40, 0) to the core — "
           + "0 is arrived.  Was 0 / 0 / 40 / 40 before plan 26 L2 (@D003).  "
           + "Got {tpd_out}");
}

fn test_and_the_robot_beside_it_arrives_every_time() {
    // ⚠ The control that makes the reading above the VEHICLE's.  Same
    // corridor, same ticks, same minute, a mover that banks — it
    // arrives at all four, so a stuck player is not a stuck world.
    arb_out   = "";
    arb_first = true;
    for arb_dt in walk_lengths() {
        if !arb_first { arb_out = arb_out + " / "; }
        arb_out   = arb_out + "{robot_walked(arb_dt)}";
        arb_first = false;
    }
    assert(arb_out == "0 / 0 / 0 / 0",
           "a robot walks the same corridor in a minute at "
           + "667 / 500 / 200 / 100 ms — got {arb_out}, where 0 is arrived");
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Why nothing in the corpus has seen it — the three accidents
//
// ⚠ These stay GREEN after L2.  They are not about the defect; they
// are about the INSTRUMENTS, and they are what stops the next reader
// concluding the existing sweeps were careless.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn test_both_shipped_vehicle_speeds_are_exact_at_the_shipped_tick() {
    // ⚠ `@M014` in a second place.  1.5 hex/s hides `enemy_bank`'s
    // epsilon; 3.0 and 6.0 hex/s hide the vehicle's missing carry — and
    // both accidents are the same one, because a vehicle speed is a
    // whole multiple of the enemy speed the tick was derived from.
    bss_cruise = VEHICLE_SPEED_HEX_PER_SECOND * TICK_SECONDS;
    bss_boost  = VEHICLE_BOOST_HEX_PER_SECOND * TICK_SECONDS;
    assert(bss_cruise == 2.0, "3.0 hex/s over today's tick is exactly 2 hexes, got {bss_cruise}");
    assert(bss_boost  == 4.0, "6.0 hex/s over today's tick is exactly 4 hexes, got {bss_boost}");
}

fn test_the_half_tick_the_corpus_already_sweeps_is_exact_too() {
    // ⚠ `tests/23_k2a::test_the_mover_agrees_at_half_the_timestep` is
    // the one gate in the repo whose axis is the tick length, and half
    // of today's tick is the one shortened timestep at which the
    // vehicle happens to be exact.  So the axis was right and the
    // reading was blind — which is not a criticism of that test but the
    // reason a sweep needs more than two points.
    thk_half = VEHICLE_SPEED_HEX_PER_SECOND * (TICK_SECONDS / 2.0);
    assert(thk_half == 1.0,
           "3.0 hex/s over half today's tick is exactly 1 hex, got {thk_half}");
}

fn test_the_tenth_tick_would_have_seen_it_and_drives_the_wrong_mover() {
    // ⚠ `23_k2a`'s other shortened timestep DID break the vehicle —
    // `3.0 * 0.0667` is 0.2 and truncates to nothing — and that test
    // banks an enemy, which carries.  This is `tests/11_f8`'s
    // markerless-world trap with the axis and the subject swapped: the
    // right sweep over a roster with none of the broken thing in it.
    //
    // ⚠ **The premise is still TRUE and only the consequence moved.**
    // A tenth of a tick is still under a whole hex for the player, so
    // a truncating mover would still read zero — what changed is that
    // there is no truncating mover.  Asserting the premise is what
    // keeps this a statement about the BLIND GATE rather than about the
    // defect, which is the half worth carrying forward.
    ttt_tenth = VEHICLE_SPEED_HEX_PER_SECOND * (TICK_SECONDS / 10.0);
    assert(ttt_tenth < 1.0,
           "3.0 hex/s over a tenth of today's tick is {ttt_tenth} hexes — "
           + "under one, so a truncating mover reads zero");
    ttt_got = vehicle_minute(false, TICK_STEP_UNITS / 10);
    assert(ttt_got == 180,
           "and a tenth-tick minute moves the player {ttt_got} hexes.  It "
           + "was 0 before plan 26 L2, at a tick length no gate in the "
           + "corpus drove a VEHICLE at");
}
